# Task context (`y-task-context`)

Use this rule when drafting **task-level** Markdown: **acceptance criteria** and **Summary** (for stakeholder review documents), and similar.

## Document title (one page with multiple sections)

When a **single document** includes **`## Summary`**, **`##` … Acceptance criteria**, and/or other sections (handoff, Confluence page, combined Markdown):

- **Set one short document title** (Confluence page title or a single `#` line in Markdown): ticket key, em dash, then a **short topic phrase** in plain language—scannable in a list, no fluff. Example: `PROJ-2717 — Ceiling water triage`. Keep it **short and reasonable**; omit file paths, commit hashes, and long subtitles.
- **Section headings are not the document title.** **`## Summary`** and **`## <KEY> — Acceptance criteria`** are **parts** of the page; they do not replace the overall title.
- **Default section order** (unless the user says otherwise): **`## Summary`** first, then **`## <KEY> — Acceptance criteria`**, then any other sections.

If the user asked for **only** AC or **only** Summary, omit the other block; a document title is optional unless they want a named page or file.

---

## Acceptance criteria (AC)

When the user asks for **acceptance criteria**, **AC**, or **definition of done** for a task or ticket, produce **Markdown** using the structure below unless they specify another format.

### Default structure

```markdown
## <KEY-123> — Acceptance criteria

### Scope

One short paragraph: what behavior or surface this work covers, who it affects, and any boundary. Stay **channel-agnostic** unless the ticket explicitly limits the channel or medium.

---

### Functional

1. **…** — Testable outcome in plain language.
2. **…** — …

---

### Non-functional / quality

1. **…** — e.g. consistency with docs, state rules, logging, performance—only what matters for *this* task.

---

### Out of scope

- **…** — What this ticket does *not* change or deliver (stops scope creep).
```

- Use the real **ticket key** in the title when known. If unknown, use `## Acceptance criteria` only.
- Use **`---`** between major sections for readability in Confluence/Jira/GitHub.

### Writing rules

- **Concise:** short bullets; one idea per numbered item where possible.
- **Testable:** each item should be verifiable (review output, QA step, automated check)—avoid vague “should work well.”
- **No unnecessary channel assumptions:** do not mention SMS, email, or app unless the task is explicitly about that channel.
- **Adapt sections:** add or rename subsections when the task needs them (e.g. **Security**, **Accessibility**, **Rollback**) but keep **Scope**, **Functional**, and usually **Out of scope** unless the user asks for a minimal list.

### When ticket context is thin

Infer scope from branch name, title, and linked PR/commits; state assumptions briefly under **Scope** or the first functional item.

---

## Summary (review and stakeholder documents)

Use this for a **stakeholder review** or **handoff document**—**not** a chat message and **not** a substitute for the AC block above unless asked to combine. On a **combined** page with AC, the **document title** is separate; this block always starts with **`## Summary`**.

### Rules

**Intro (top of Summary, before `### Problems`):**

- **Keep it brief.** Do not restate long mandatory-review boilerplate.
- **Document voice:** neutral, third person or institutional—**not** conversational (no “Hi,” “thanks,” direct address).
- State **what the document summarizes** and, if useful, **what kind of feedback** is wanted—in one to three short sentences.

**Work item:**

- Put **`Work item:`** with a **Markdown link** to the tracker when the key is known: `[KEY-123](https://<YOUR-JIRA-SITE>/browse/KEY-123)`. Place it **after** the intro paragraph and **before** **`### Problems`**. If the site or key is unknown, use plain text or ask once.

**Problems / Improvements:**

- **Concise:** short bullets; easy to scan.
- **Audience:** default to **operations / PM / domain expert**—avoid file paths, commit hashes, and internal code names unless asked.
- Use **`### Problems`** and **`### Improvements`**.

### Template (default)

```markdown
## Summary

This document summarizes the **…** changes regarding **…**, and what to look for when reviewing them.

**Work item:** [PROJ-2717](https://your-org.atlassian.net/browse/PROJ-2717)

### Problems

- …
- …

### Improvements

- …
- …
```

Replace the example key/URL with the real issue. Ask the user for their Jira site if not known from context or team config.
